        </div>
        <div class="dm-help">Safety backups are local-only and do not consume slots.</div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="new-device">Moving to a new device</h2>
    <p>
      Your BudgetBaby data does not transfer to a new iPhone automatically. There are two
      ways to move it. Most people should use the migration file method below.
    </p>

    <h3 id="migration-file">Method 1: Migration file (recommended)</h3>
    <p>
      A migration file is a single <code>.bbbackup</code> file that contains your entire
      BudgetBaby dataset, encrypted with a passphrase you set. It works no matter what
      Apple ID or iCloud account is on the new device, the only thing that matters is
      the passphrase.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>On your old iPhone, go to <strong>Settings → Backup &amp; Restore</strong> and find the Migration File section. Tap <strong>Save Backup to File</strong>.</li>
      <li>Set a passphrase you will remember. Tap <strong>Export</strong>. Share the resulting <code>.bbbackup</code> file via AirDrop, iMessage, email, iCloud Drive, or any file transfer you prefer.</li>
      <li>Install BudgetBaby on the new iPhone.</li>
      <li>On the new device, open <strong>Settings → Backup &amp; Restore</strong> and tap the Migration File <strong>Import</strong> action. Select the <code>.bbbackup</code> you transferred. Enter the passphrase. Restore.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>Why this is the most reliable method.</strong> The encryption key is the
      passphrase you choose, embedded in the file. Nothing depends on iCloud Keychain
      sync or Apple IDs matching. It also works for moving between two different
      App Store versions of BudgetBaby (for example, between an older personal build
      and the current LLC build).
    </div>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Export migration file" caption="Wrap your dataset in a single .bbbackup file with a passphrase you choose.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-back">Settings</span><span class="dm-bar-title">Migration</span><span class="dm-bar-action">Export</span></div>
        <div class="dm-card dm-card-accent" style="text-align:center;">
          <div class="dm-hero-label">budgetbaby_2026-05-03.bbbackup</div>
          <div class="dm-hero-amount" style="font-size:18px;">2.4 MB</div>
          <div class="dm-hero-sub">AES-256-GCM, sealed with your passphrase</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-section-label">Passphrase</div>
        <div class="dm-card" style="font-family:var(--font-mono); letter-spacing:0.4em; font-size:12px; text-align:center; padding:8px;">••••••••••</div>
        <div class="dm-help">Pick something memorable. We never see this, you cannot recover it later.</div>
        <div class="dm-btn dm-btn-primary">Export & Share…</div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h3 id="cloud-restore">Method 2: Cloud backup + iCloud Keychain</h3>
    <p>
      If you have not created a migration file but you do have a recent cloud backup
      and both devices share the same iCloud account:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>On your old iPhone, go to <strong>Settings → Backup &amp; Restore</strong> and tap <strong>Create Backup</strong>.</li>
      <li>Install BudgetBaby on your new iPhone.</li>
      <li>Sign in to the same BudgetBaby account on the new device: Settings → Account.</li>
      <li>Make sure iCloud Keychain is enabled on the new device: iOS Settings → (your name) → iCloud → Keychain.</li>
      <li>Open BudgetBaby → Settings → Backup &amp; Restore. Your backup should appear in the list within a minute or two as the iCloud Keychain sync completes.</li>
      <li>Tap the backup and restore from it.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>Why the wait for iCloud Keychain?</strong> The decryption key is stored in
      your Keychain, not in the backup itself. Until iCloud Keychain syncs the key to
      your new device, the backup is downloaded but cannot be decrypted. Give it a few
      minutes after enabling iCloud Keychain before attempting the restore.
    </div>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Waiting for Keychain" caption="A new device decrypts only after iCloud Keychain delivers your key.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-back">Account</span><span class="dm-bar-title">Backup</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="dm-card dm-card-warn" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="dm-icon lock" style="width:30px; height:30px; margin:0 auto;"><span class="dm-glyph-lock"></span></span>
          <div class="dm-h2" style="margin-top:6px;">Waiting for key</div>
          <div class="dm-help" style="text-align:center; color:#FDBA74;">iCloud Keychain is syncing. Restore unlocks once the key arrives.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-card">
          <div class="dm-kv"><span><span style="color:#34D399;">✓</span> Account signed in</span><span class="muted"></span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span><span style="color:#34D399;">✓</span> Backup downloaded</span><span class="muted">2.4 MB</span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span><span style="color:#FBBF24;">●</span> Encryption key syncing…</span><span class="muted">~1 min</span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span><span style="color:var(--text-faint);">○</span> Restore</span><span class="muted">pending</span></div>
        </div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="keychain-loss">If you lose iCloud Keychain access</h2>
    <p>
      This is the most serious data recovery scenario. If you cannot access your iCloud Keychain
      (for example, if you were not using iCloud Keychain and lost your old device with no replacement),
      the encryption key needed to decrypt your backups may not be recoverable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because we designed the encryption so that only you hold the key, we cannot decrypt backups on
      your behalf even if you contact us. This is a deliberate privacy guarantee, not an accident.
    </p>
    <p>
      Steps to try before concluding the key is gone:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Check whether your old iPhone still turns on. The Keychain key is on the device. You can sign in on the new device, enable iCloud Keychain, and let the old device sync the key while both devices are on the same iCloud account.</li>
      <li>Check if you have an iPhone device backup from before the loss. Restoring that backup to a device and enabling BudgetBaby will restore the local Keychain, including the encryption key.</li>
      <li>Contact Apple Support about Keychain recovery options. In some cases Apple can help recover iCloud Keychain contents through their account recovery process.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If the key truly cannot be recovered, your cloud backups are not accessible. However, if you
      have a new device and re-install BudgetBaby, you can start a new account and begin fresh.
      For future protection, always enable iCloud Keychain on all your devices.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Recovery options" caption="Try the full recovery sequence before assuming the key is lost.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-back">Account</span><span class="dm-bar-title">Recovery</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="dm-card">
          <div class="dm-kv"><span class="dm-pill purple">1</span><strong>Old iPhone still on?</strong></div>
          <div class="dm-help">Sign in on it and let iCloud Keychain re-sync the key.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-card">
          <div class="dm-kv"><span class="dm-pill purple">2</span><strong>iPhone device backup</strong></div>
          <div class="dm-help">Restore an older iPhone backup that contains the Keychain.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-card">
          <div class="dm-kv"><span class="dm-pill purple">3</span><strong>Apple account recovery</strong></div>
          <div class="dm-help">Apple may be able to restore Keychain via account recovery.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-card dm-card-error">
          <div class="dm-kv"><strong>If all fail</strong><span class="dm-pill red">!</span></div>
          <div class="dm-help" style="color:#FCA5A5;">By design, we cannot decrypt for you. Start fresh on the new device.</div>
        </div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="managing-backups">Managing and deleting backups</h2>
    <p>To view, manage, or delete your backups:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to <strong>Settings → Backup &amp; Restore</strong>.</li>
      <li>Swipe left on any backup in the list to reveal the Delete action. Tap <strong>Delete</strong> and confirm.</li>
      <li>To delete every cloud backup at once, scroll to the bottom and tap the destructive Delete-All action; confirm in the alert sheet.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="doc-warning">
      <strong>Backup deletion is permanent and cannot be undone.</strong> Because your backups are encrypted with a key only your device holds, neither you nor we can recover a deleted backup. The server-side encrypted blob and the on-device key are both required; deleting the blob makes the backup unrecoverable even if the key still exists.
    </div>
    <p>
      For complete instructions on deleting your account and all associated data, see the
      <a href="/budgetbaby/delete-data">Delete Your Data</a> page.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Manage Backups" caption="Swipe to delete; the Delete All button clears every cloud slot at once.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-back">Account</span><span class="dm-bar-title">Manage Backups</span><span class="dm-bar-action">Edit</span></div>
        <div class="dm-list">
          <div class="dm-row" style="position:relative; padding-right:50px;">
